  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nWith an expansive upper ridge occupying the southeastern third of \nthe country, well above normal temperatures continue to be the \nprimary concern through the entire forecast. The core of this upper \nridge will shift east over the next couple of days, which may lead \nto a degree or two cooler high temperatures. This is still 10-15 \ndegrees above normal for this time of year. So overall, little, if \nany, relief to the heat is expected through the end of the week,\nand quite possibly through the start of next week as well. The \nheat advisory will likely need to be extended in time but there \nis some uncertainty about the locations. Given that is still day\n5 of the forecast, will hold off for now and continue to \nmessage the heat through the DSS packet. Looking at ensemble \nguidance and 500mb clusters, the pattern is overall little \nchanged as the upper ridge builds back into and expands across \nthe middle of the country. By days 6 and 7 (Sunday and Monday), \na few of the ensembles (generally 5-10%) are showing some easing\nof the ridging in the middle of the country with some negative \ndifferences appearing between those clusters relative to the \nensemble mean. This is still very unlikely to occur but what's a\nprolonged heat wave without some hope that it will end... \neventually. \n\nRegarding precipitation chances, it's unlikely we'll see much of \nanything through the end of the week. By Sunday, there is at least a \nsmall chance in our northeastern zones as the ridge is in the \nprocess of expanding across the middle of the country again. That \nsaid, the prevailing theme remains hot with little, if any, \nprecipitation through the entire forecast.",
